theory.intro:
A 3-D object looks different from different directions. We can draw its **top**, **front** and **side** views as flat 2-D shapes.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3,0);
\coordinate (C) at (3,1.8);
\coordinate (D) at (0,1.8);
\coordinate (E) at (1.212,0.7);
\coordinate (F) at (4.212,0.7);
\coordinate (G) at (4.212,2.5);
\coordinate (H) at (1.212,2.5);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (B)--(F)--(G)--(C);
\draw (D)--(H)--(G);
\draw[dashed] (A)--(E)--(F);
\draw[dashed] (E)--(H);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (1.5,-1.6)--(1.5,-0.35);
\node[below,font=\small] at (1.5,-1.6) {Front};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (2.7,4.2)--(2.7,3.0);
\node[above,font=\small] at (2.7,4.2) {Top};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (6.6,1.2)--(4.6,1.2);
\node[right,font=\small] at (6.6,1.2) {Side};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
